<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hertfordshire</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hertfordshire is a county of south-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, immediately north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, with Hertford as its county town. In the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> it was the heartland of the Catuvellauni, and the Roman town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verulamium">Verulamium</a> stood there beside modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-albans">St Albans</a>. After the Roman period it was settled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> and divided between Mercia and the East Saxons.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hertfordshire.jpg" alt="Hertfordshire" width="320" height="182" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Peter O'Connor aka anemoneprojectors · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:GOC_Hexton_007_Chilterns_landscape_(24372234911)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Hertfordshire is a ceremonial county in the East of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. It borders Bedfordshire to the north-west, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridgeshire">Cambridgeshire</a> to the north-east, Essex to the east, Greater London to the south and Buckinghamshire to the west, and covers about 1,640 square kilometres. It centres on the headwaters and upper valleys of the rivers Lea and Colne, both of which flow south. The higher ground lies in the north and west, where the chalk of the Chiltern Hills rises to 244 metres near Tring.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Human life is attested in the county from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesolithic">Mesolithic</a> period. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-conquest-of-britain">Roman conquest of Britain</a> in 43 CE the Catuvellauni accepted peace and adapted to Roman life, and new towns developed, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verulamium">Verulamium</a>, beside modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-albans">St Albans</a>. There, around 293, the first recorded British martyrdom is traditionally believed to have taken place, when Saint Alban, a Romano-British soldier, took the place of a Christian priest and was beheaded. After the Roman legions left in the early 5th century the area was colonised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxons</a>, and by the 6th century most of it belonged to the Kingdom of Essex, which later lost its western areas to Mercia.</p><p>In 913 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-elder">Edward the Elder</a> built a fortress at Hertford to check Norse activity, and the area was assigned to it. His father, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-the-great">Alfred the Great</a>, had fixed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-lea">River Lea</a> as a boundary with the Norse lord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guthrum">Guthrum</a>, which put the north and east of the county within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danelaw">Danelaw</a>. The name Hertfordshire is first recorded in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxon-chronicle">Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</a> in 1011. St Albans Abbey was an early drafting place of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magna-carta">Magna Carta</a>, and the town saw two battles of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wars-of-the-roses">Wars of the Roses</a>. The New River, opened in 1613, brought fresh water to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> from Ware. In 1903 Letchworth became the first garden city, and Stevenage was the first town redeveloped under the New Towns Act 1946.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Hertfordshire has ten districts, with Hertford as county town and Watford as its largest settlement, and about 1.2 million people in 2021. Much of its undeveloped land is farmland and part of the Metropolitan green belt. Among its surviving landmarks are the Roman remains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verulamium">Verulamium</a>, St Albans Cathedral, Berkhamsted Castle and the Jacobean Hatfield House.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hertfordshire” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the second battle fought at St Albans, <b>Hertfordshire</b>, the Lancastrian army defeated the Yorkist army and released King Henry VI from captivity.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-17">17 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Because their name resembles that of Cassivellaunus, some historians suggest they were his own people or a group within his following north of the Thames, perhaps in <b>Hertfordshire</b>, but there is no firm evidence.</blockquote><a href="/state/cassi">Cassi</a></li><li><blockquote>The Catuvellauni held the lands north of the Thames around <b>Hertfordshire</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/catuvellauni">Catuvellauni</a></li><li><blockquote>The East Saxons settled Essex, Middlesex and parts of <b>Hertfordshire</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-essex">Kingdom of Essex</a></li></ul></section>
